   "widgets_values": [
    "## Outpaint a fragment (big sources: extend part of the picture)\n\nSame px knobs and factor math as `outpaint-px.json` (`f = 1 + N/W`, source-relative) - plus a **mask**: paint (MaskEditor) the **edge strip** to be extended + repainted. The mask does two jobs: it **localizes the crop** (so a big source samples only a local region, in the SDXL budget) and it marks the strip that becomes the **transition** into the new area (the strip is repainted - keep it thin). **`context_from_mask_extend_factor` is 4.0 here, not the pack default 1.2** - measured: at 1.2 the crop is a ~100 px sliver and the model invents in isolation (E41: it built a decontextualized second cottage); at 4.0 the crop carries enough scene to continue coherently.\n\n- Same budget note: the output canvas is `W + N_left + N_right` x `H + N_up + N_down` at the **source resolution** - the pack edge-replicates, samples the local crop natively, and stitches.\n- Never pad with **black**: base SDXL will not paint over black padding (E41 - strips came back black at denoise 0.9 and 1.0). The pack's own extend padding is edge replication - let it pad.\n- For extending a **whole small picture**, use `outpaint-px.json`.\n\n*How to run it: story **S5** in `HOWTO.md`  \u00b7  Why it is this way: **E41** in `2d/EXPERIMENTS-2D.md`.*\n\n**Very large sources (4K+) - the marching recipe (for revisiting):**\n- mask **~300x300 square-ish patches touching the edge** (bottom corner of the patch ON the canvas edge, so the 4x-grown crop reaches into the extension area; square-ish keeps the crop aspect sane - a long thin strip mask gives a 4:1 crop, which SDXL composes poorly even when it fits the MP budget)\n- context 4.0, constant **N** = the extension depth; **~13 runs per 3840 px edge**, feed-forward: each run's stitched output is the next run's input\n- prompt per patch = the **local continuation** (trap 3)\n- trade knob: context 2.0 doubles the patch size (~600x600, ~7 runs per edge) as long as the crop keeps **>= ~150 px of real scene per side** and aspect <= ~1:2.5\n- one mask = one local region; opposite edges = separate runs\n- source size is unconstrained - only the crop must fit the budget; a batch driver walking a mask grid would automate the march if this ever gets tedious"
   ],
   "widgets_values_named": {
    "text": "## Outpaint a fragment (big sources: extend part of the picture)\n\nSame px knobs and factor math as `outpaint-px.json` (`f = 1 + N/W`, source-relative) - plus a **mask**: paint (MaskEditor) the **edge strip** to be extended + repainted. The mask does two jobs: it **localizes the crop** (so a big source samples only a local region, in the SDXL budget) and it marks the strip that becomes the **transition** into the new area (the strip is repainted - keep it thin). **`context_from_mask_extend_factor` is 4.0 here, not the pack default 1.2** - measured: at 1.2 the crop is a ~100 px sliver and the model invents in isolation (E41: it built a decontextualized second cottage); at 4.0 the crop carries enough scene to continue coherently.\n\n- Same budget note: the output canvas is `W + N_left + N_right` x `H + N_up + N_down` at the **source resolution** - the pack edge-replicates, samples the local crop natively, and stitches.\n- Never pad with **black**: base SDXL will not paint over black padding (E41 - strips came back black at denoise 0.9 and 1.0). The pack's own extend padding is edge replication - let it pad.\n- For extending a **whole small picture**, use `outpaint-px.json`.\n\n*How to run it: story **S5** in `HOWTO.md`  \u00b7  Why it is this way: **E41** in `2d/EXPERIMENTS-2D.md`.*\n\n**Very large sources (4K+) - the marching recipe (for revisiting):**\n- mask **~300x300 square-ish patches touching the edge** (bottom corner of the patch ON the canvas edge, so the 4x-grown crop reaches into the extension area; square-ish keeps the crop aspect sane - a long thin strip mask gives a 4:1 crop, which SDXL composes poorly even when it fits the MP budget)\n- context 4.0, constant **N** = the extension depth; **~13 runs per 3840 px edge**, feed-forward: each run's stitched output is the next run's input\n- prompt per patch = the **local continuation** (trap 3)\n- trade knob: context 2.0 doubles the patch size (~600x600, ~7 runs per edge) as long as the crop keeps **>= ~150 px of real scene per side** and aspect <= ~1:2.5\n- one mask = one local region; opposite edges = separate runs\n- source size is unconstrained - only the crop must fit the budget; a batch driver walking a mask grid would automate the march if this ever gets tedious"
   },
